<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/advanced/environmental-design-light-heat-air">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Environmental design is <strong>buildings that harvest their climate instead of fighting
    it</strong> — sun, daylight, wind and thermal mass arranged by geometry before machinery is
    even specified. It is where the geography lesson\'s climate maps become architecture.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="sun control, daylight, natural ventilation, the envelope" widget="equationStep" config={envDesignCfg} />

  <h2>Evidence-based design arrives</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Simulation is routine:</strong> daylight analysis, CFD airflow, energy modelling on every serious project — the climate-modelling lesson\'s tools at building scale; design decisions now come with numbers (the evidence movement the medicine lesson documented, arriving in architecture).</li>
    <li><strong>Comfort is broader than 22 °C:</strong> adaptive comfort research shows people comfortable across a range IF they control their environment (openable windows beat perfect mechanical air) — the psychology lesson\'s agency finding, thermally.</li>
    <li><strong>Sick building syndrome taught ventilation humility:</strong> 1970s airtight buildings under-ventilated — indoor air quality (CO₂, VOCs) became measurable and regulated; fresh air is a budgeted design quantity (the air-quality lesson, indoors).</li>
    <li><strong>The window–wall tension:</strong> daylight and views want glass; heat control wants opacity — the trade is quantified now (WWR optimisation), and dynamic glass (electrochromic) is the technology trying to abolish the trade-off.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Olgyay, Design with Climate (1963); Szokolay, Introduction to Architectural Science; the passive-design lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
